ive" style="background:#ffe0b2" data-tip="unigénito" data-lemma="μονογενής" data-morph="A-NSM" data-strongs="G3439">μονογενής</td><td class="tc-word-cell" style="background:#eee"></td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="unigénito" data-lemma="μονογενής" data-morph="A-NSM" data-strongs="G3439">μονογενὴς</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="dios" data-lemma="θεός" data-morph="N-NSM" data-strongs="G2316">Θεὸς</td></tr></table></div><div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#555;margin:0.3rem 0;padding:0.3rem 0.5rem;background:#f5f5f5;border-radius:4px"><strong>Lecturas:</strong> <span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#1: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">μονογενὴς θεός</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#2: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">ὁ μονογενὴς θεός</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#3: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">ὁ μονογενὴς υἱός</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#4: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">μονογενὴς υἱὸς θεοῦ</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#5: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">ὁ μονογενής</span></span></div><div style="font-size:0.63rem;color:#999;margin-top:2px;display:flex;gap:0.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><span style="font-weight:600">MSS:</span> <span>🔴 Papiros</span> <span>🔵 Unciales</span> <span>⚪ Minúsc.</span> <span>🟣 Versiones</span> <span>🟠 Padres</span> <span>🟢 Byz/Lect</span></div><div class="tc-criteria-expanded"><div class="tc-criterion-item"><strong>Atestación temprana</strong><div class="tc-criterion-exp">Los manuscritos papiráceos más antiguos (P66, P75) y los unciales tempranos apoyan 'θεός'. Esto sugiere que esta lectura más difícil es original.</div></div><div class="tc-criterion-item"><strong>Lectio difficilior</strong><div class="tc-criterion-exp">La expresión 'μονογενὴς θεός' es más inusual y teológicamente audaz que 'μονογενὴς υἱός'. Los escribas habrían tendido a cambiar hacia la expresión más familiar 'Hijo'.</div></div></div><div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#1565c0;margin-top:0.5rem;cursor:pointer" onclick="document.getElementById('exeg-18')?.classList.remove('collapsed');document.getElementById('exeg-18')?.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">↑ Ver análisis exegético de este versículo</div><div style="margin-top:0.8rem;text-align:center"><a href="tc_deep_dive_John_1_18.html" onclick="if(!this.dataset.ready){this.textContent='⏳ Generando análisis profundo...';this.style.opacity=0.6;return true;}" style="display:inline-block;padding:8px 20px;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#1a237e,#0d47a1);color:white;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:600;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(26,35,126,0.3)">🔍 Deep Dive — Análisis completo de esta variante</a><div style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#888;margin-top:3px">Si la página no existe, usa el tool <code>critical_apparatus</code> → <code>tc_deep_dive</code> en el MCP server para generarla.</div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb19"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">19</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-19">Καὶ αὕτη ἐστὶν ἡ μαρτυρία τοῦ Ἰωάννου ὅτε ἀπέστειλαν πρὸς αὐτὸν οἱ Ἰουδαῖοι ἐξ Ἱεροσολύμων ἱερεῖς καὶ Λευίτας ἵνα ἐρωτήσωσιν αὐτόν Σὺ τίς εἶ</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-19" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΚΑΙ·ΑΥΤΗ·ΕΣΤΙΝ·Η·ΜΑΡΤΥΡΙΑ·ΤΟΥ·ΙΩΑΝΝΟΥ·ΟΤΕ·ΑΠΕΣΤΕΙΛΑΝ·ΠΡΟΣ·ΑΥΤΟΝ·ΟΙ·ΙΟΥΔΑΙΟΙ·ΕΞ·ΙΕΡΟΣΟΛΥΜΩΝ·ΙΕΡΕΙΣ·ΚΑΙ·ΛΕΥΙΤΑΣ·ΙΝΑ·ΕΡΩΤΗΣΩΣΙΝ·ΑΥΤΟΝ·ΣΥ·ΤΙΣ·ΕΙ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=19" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(19)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-19').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-19').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-19').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-19').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-19')" title="15 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 15 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-19')" title="3 comentarios exegéticos">📚 3 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-19').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(19)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-19').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-19').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-19" class="collapsed spanish-line">Este es el testimonio de Juan, cuando los judíos enviaron de Jerusalén sacerdotes y levitas para que le preguntasen: ¿Tú, quién eres?</div><div id="vers-19" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>Este es el testimonio de Juan, cuando los judíos enviaron de Jerusalén sacerdotes y levitas para que le preguntasen: ¿Tú, quién eres?</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>Y éste es el testimonio de Juan, cuando los Judíos enviaron de Jerusalem sacerdotes y Levitas, que le preguntasen: ¿Tú, quién eres?</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>And this is the record of John, when the Jews sent priests and Levites from Jerusalem to ask him, Who art thou?</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>And this is the witness of John, when the Jews sent unto him from Jerusalem priests and Levites to ask him, Who art thou? </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>  And this was John’s testimony when the Jews of Jerusalem sent priests and Levites to ask him, “Who are you?” </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>And this is the witness of John, when the Jews sent from Jerusalem priests and Levites that they might ask him, Thou, who art thou? </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>And this is the witness of John, when the Jews sent priests and Levites that they might ask him, Who are you? </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>And this is the testimony of John, when the Jews sent out of Jerusalem priests and Levites, that they might question him, `Who art thou?'</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>et hoc est testimonium Iohannis quando miserunt Iudaei ab Hierosolymis sacerdotes et Levitas ad eum ut interrogarent eum tu quis es</div></div><div id="pron-19" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-19" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-19" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.19..." oninput="saveNote(19)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-19')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-19" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-19">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-19" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Alford:</strong> ] αὕτη is the predicate, ἡ μαρτυρία the subject, in the present form of the sentence.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> Οἰ ἰουδᾶιοι , the Jews ) Matthew, Mark, and Luke rarely employ the appellation Jews ; John most frequently: no doubt the cause is, they supposed, as their first readers, Jews: John, believers of the G…<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> Final ινα and the first aorist active subjunctive of ερωταω , old verb to ask a question as here and often in the Koine to ask for something ([John 14:16]) like αιτεω.<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv19_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Alford's Greek Testament</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1863)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv19_0" class="collapsed comm-body">19. ] αὕτη is the predicate, ἡ μαρτυρία the subject, in the present form of the sentence. So very frequently in St. John, where commonly the mistake is made of supposing the demonstrative pronoun to be the subject, whereas it is ever the predicate of identification. Euthym [25] , αὕτη … περὶ ἧς εἰπεῖν μέλλει προϊών , … ἡ γενομένη δηλονότι ὅτε ἀπεστ . κ . τ . λ . [25] Euthymius Zigabenus, 1116 οἱ Ἰουδαῖοι ] John alone of the Evangelists uses this expression; principally as designating the chiefs of the Jewish people , the members of the Sanhedrim. It is an interesting enquiry, what this usage denotes as to the author or date of our Gospel. Prof. Bleek, Beiträge, pp. 245 249, has satisfactorily shewn that no inference can be deduced from it against the Jewish origin of the author, as Bretschneider and Fischer endeavoured to do: but it is rather confirmatory of the belief that the Gospel was written after the Jews had ceased to be politically a nation, and among Gentiles; the author himself contemplating these last as his readers. ἐξ Ἱερ . does not belong to οἱ Ἰουδ ., nor to ἱερ . κ . Λευ ., but to ἀπέστειλαν : sent from Jerusalem priests, &c.: so ἐξαποστέλλω , [Acts 7:12] ; Act 11:22 alli [26] . [26] alli = some cursive mss. ἱερ . κ . Λ .] This was a formal deputation; priests and Levites, constituting the two classes of persons employed about the service of the temple (see Jos 3:3 ), are sent ( Mat 21:23 ) officially to enquire into the pretensions of the new Teacher ( Joh 1:25 ), who had collected about him such multitudes ( Mat 3:5 ), and had awakened popular expectation that he was the Messiah ( Luk 3:5 ). σὺ τίς εἶ ; with reference to the popular doubts respecting him; asked in an unbelieving and inquisitorial spirit, compare [Matthew 3:7] ff., which had already taken place. Even among the learned, as well as among the people, there were considerable differences as to the prophecies respecting the Messiah: see ch. [John 7:40-52] .</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv19_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv19_1" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:19] . Οἰ ἰουδᾶιοι , the Jews ) Matthew, Mark, and Luke rarely employ the appellation Jews ; John most frequently: no doubt the cause is, they supposed, as their first readers, Jews: John, believers of the Gentiles. ἐξ ιἑροσολύμων , from Jerusalem ) that seat of religion. ιἑρεῖς καὶ λευίτας , priests and Levites ) With the testimony of John to the people is interwoven his testimony to the rulers. This embassy, sent forty days at least after the baptism of Jesus [to allow for the forty days&rsquo; temptation subsequent to the baptism], indicates, that the preaching of John began not at a long interval before the baptism of Jesus. Otherwise the embassy would have been sent earlier. ἐρωτήσωσιν , that they should ask ) in the public name, ch. [John 5:33] [Ye sent unto John, and he bare witness unto the truth]. σὺ τὶς εἶ ; who art thou? ) with that baptism of thine, [John 1:25] . [Why baptizeth thou then?]</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv19_2').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv19_2" class="collapsed comm-body">And this is the witness of John ( κα αυτη εστιν η μαρτυρια του Ιωανου ). He had twice already alluded to it (verses [John 1:7]; [John 1:15]) and now he proceeds to give it as the most important item to add after the Prologue. Just as the author assumes the birth narratives of Matthew and Luke, so he assumes the Synoptic accounts of the baptism of Jesus by John, but adds various details of great interest and value between the baptism and the Galilean ministry, filling out thus our knowledge of this first year of the Lord's ministry in various parts of Palestine. The story in John proceeds along the same lines as in the Synoptics. There is increasing unfolding of Christ to the disciples with increasing hostility on the part of the Jews till the final consummation in Jerusalem. When the Jews sent unto him ( οτε απεστειλαν προς αυτον ο Ιουδαιο ). John, writing in Ephesus near the close of the first century long after the destruction of Jerusalem, constantly uses the phrase "the Jews" as descriptive of the people as distinct from the Gentile world and from the followers of Christ (at first Jews also). Often he uses it of the Jewish leaders and rulers in particular who soon took a hostile attitude toward both John and Jesus. Here it is the Jews from Jerusalem who sent ( απεστειλαν , first aorist active indicative of αποστελλω ). Priests and Levites ( ιερεις κα Λευειτας ). Sadducees these were. Down below in verse [John 1:24] the author explains that it was the Pharisees who sent the Sadducees. The Synoptics throw a flood of light on this circumstance, for in [Matthew 3:7] we are told that the Baptist called the Pharisees and Sadducees "offspring of vipers" ([Luke 3:7]). Popular interest in John grew till people were wondering "in their hearts concerning John whether haply he were the Christ" ([Luke 3:15]). So the Sanhedrin finally sent a committee to John to get his own view of himself, but the Pharisees saw to it that Sadducees were sent. To ask him ( ινα ερωτησωσιν αυτον ). Final ινα and the first aorist active subjunctive of ερωταω , old verb to ask a question as here and often in the Koine to ask for something ([John 14:16]) like αιτεω . Who art thou? ( συ τις ει; ). Direct question preserved and note proleptic position of συ , "Thou, who art thou?" The committee from the Sanhedrin put the question sharply up to John to define his claims concerning the Messiah.</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-19')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-19" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 3 temas · 15 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-19">▶</span></div><div id="patr-19" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt0d7222a2').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Juan el Bautista como precursor y heraldo de Cristo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt0d7222a2" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (c.354-430) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.19]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"John the Baptist, in proportion as he was greater than those born of women, and was more humble in his acknowledgment of the Lord, obtained the grace of being the friend of the Bridegroom; zealous for the Bridegroom, not for himself; not seeking his own honor, but that of his Judge, whom as a herald he preceded"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Gregory the Dialogist</strong> (c.540-604) <span class="patr-meta">— Papa, reformador, Doctor de la Iglesia (Roma)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorio_I" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.23]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"John declares himself to be the voice, i. e. because he precedes the Word, and, through his ministry, the Word of the Father is heard by man"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> (c.1225-1274) <span class="patr-meta">— Teólogo escolástico, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Alberto Magno (Italia)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom%C3%A1s_de_Aquino" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.23]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"God the Father sent the precursor John, who came to be in time, in order to make known his Word, which was conceived from eternity. And so he fittingly says, I am a voice"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres concuerdan unánimemente en que Juan el Bautista fue enviado como precursor y heraldo de Cristo. Su función era preparar el camino y dar testimonio del Verbo divino.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt866fef83').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>La envidia y malicia de los judíos hacia Juan</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt866fef83" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (c.349-407) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.19]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"A Dreadful thing is envy, beloved, a dreadful thing and a pernicious, to the enviers, not to the envied. All this I have said, by reason of the envy of the Jews"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (c.349-407) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.19]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"When then John continually sent them from himself to Jesus, who seemed to them a meaner person, being ashamed and vexed at this, and wishing rather to have him for their teacher, they did not dare to say so plainly, but send to him, thinking by their flattery to induce him to confess that he was the Christ"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres interpretan las preguntas de los judíos como motivadas por envidia y malicia. Buscaban halagar a Juan para que se proclamara el Cristo, rechazando así a Jesús.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt67631527').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>La autoridad de los enviados desde Jerusalén</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:55%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#ff9800">medio</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt67631527" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Origen of Alexandria</strong> (c.185-254) <span class="patr-meta">— Maestro catequético, exégeta, discípulo de Clemente de Alejandría (Alejandría/Cesarea)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Or%C3%ADgenes" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.19]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Note that two embassies come to the Baptist. One consists of 'priests and Levites' sent from Jerusalem by the Jews 'to ask him, Who are you?' The other comes from the Pharisees, who send also because they are in doubt about the answer that had been given to the priests and Levites"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (c.349-407) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.19]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"They do not therefore send to him mean men, as in the case of Christ, for when they wished to lay hold on Him, they sent servants, and then Herodians, and the like, but in this instance, 'priests and Levites'"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres notan la importancia de que enviaran sacerdotes y levitas de alto rango a interrogar a Juan, contrastando con el trato inferior dado posteriormente a Cristo.</div></div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('tc-19')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="tc-19" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">⚖️</span> Crítica Textual <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#ff9800">(3 lecturas)</span> <span class="arrow" id="arrow-tc-19">▶</span></div><div id="tc-19" class="collapsed section-content tc-section"><div class="tc-verdict" style="margin-bottom:0.8rem"><div class="tc-verdict-text"><strong>Veredicto:</strong> La lectura #6.3 que omite 'πρὸς αὐτόν' es probablemente original, apoyada por manuscritos tempranos como P66*, P75, א, C3, L. Las lecturas #6.1 y #6.2 que incluyen la frase en diferentes posiciones representan adiciones escribales para clarificar a quién se dirigieron los judíos. La variación en la posición de la frase (#6.1 vs #6.2) sugiere que es una adición secundaria insertada en diferentes lugares por diferentes escribas.</div><div class="consensus-bar" style="max-width:200px"><div style="width:60%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#ff9800;font-weight:600;margin-left:4px">Confianza: media</span><div class="tc-impact"><strong>Impacto:</strong> La presencia o ausencia de 'πρὸς αὐτόν' afecta la claridad narrativa sobre a quién se dirigieron los líderes judíos. Sin embargo, el contexto hace suficientemente claro que se dirigieron a Juan el Bautista, por lo que la adición es estilística más que sustancial.</div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><table class="tc-table"><tr class="tc-header"><th class="tc-ms-col">MS</th><th class="tc-ctx">ὅτε</th><th class="tc-ctx">ἀπέστειλαν</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 1: πρὸς">⚡1</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 2: αὐτόν">⚡2</th><th class="tc-ctx">οἱ</th><th class="tc-ctx">Ἰουδαῖοι</th></tr><tr><td class="tc-ms-cell" onclick="showMSSPanel('B C* 33 892c 1010 1071 ita, aur, b, c syrc, p, pal copsa, bo arm eth geo slav Chrysostomlem Cyril')"><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#1565c0;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="B" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'B');">B</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="33" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'33');">33</span><span class="ms-chip" style="" data-s="892c" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'892c');">892c</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="1010" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'1010');">1010</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="1071" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'1071');">1071</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#f3e5f5;color:#6a1b9a" data-s="ita" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'ita');">ita</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#f3e5f5;color:#6a1b9a" data-s="aur" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'aur');">aur</span><span class="ms-chip" style="" data-s="b" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'b');">b</span><span class="ms-chip" style="" data-s="c" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'c');">c</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#f3e5f5;color:#6a1b9a" dat